content.thinking:
The user wants: the timeline supports collapsing/expanding by year, with the most recent year expanded by default.

Current TimelineView structure:
- Years render always expanded (all months + photos shown)
- Months have expand/collapse for photos (COLLAPSED_COUNT=12)

New requirement: **year-level collapse/expand**, default: only the most recent year expanded, others collapsed.

Implementation:
1. Add `expandedYears` ref — default `[firstYear]` (most recent year, since timeline is sorted DESC by year)
2. `isYearExpanded(year)` / `toggleYear(year)` functions
3. Template: wrap the months section in `v-if="isYearExpanded(group.year)"`, add a toggle button/chevron on the year header
4. Style: chevron rotates when expanded; collapsed year shows just the header

Design considerations:
- Year header currently: dot + "2026 年" + count badge. Add a chevron (▶/▼) at the right end, clickable. Make the whole year header clickable (cursor pointer).
- When collapsed, show the year header only (with count). Maybe show a hint of months count? Keep simple: just year + count + chevron.
- Default: `expandedYears` initialized after timeline loads — the first (most recent) year. Since `timeline` loads async in onMounted, initialize expandedYears there: `expandedYears.value = [timeline.value[0]?.year]` (guard for empty).

Note the interplay with month expansion: when a year is collapsed and re-expanded, month expand states persist (expandedMonths untouched) — fine.

Also the vertical timeline line: when years are collapsed, the line still spans the container (::before with top:0 bottom:0) — fine, it connects the year dots.

Edge case: empty timeline → no default year. Guard with `?.[0]`.

Let me also think about whether the user might want the year header to be the click target — yes, natural. Add chevron indicator.

Let me implement:

```js
const expandedYears = ref([])

onMounted(async () => {
  timeline.value = await mediaStore.fetchTimeline()
  await loadAllMedia()
  // 默认只展开最近一年
  if (timeline.value.length) {
    expandedYears.value = [timeline.value[0].year]
  }
})

function isYearExpanded(year) {
  return expandedYears.value.includes(year)
}

function toggleYear(year) {
  const idx = expandedYears.value.indexOf(year)
  if (idx > -1) expandedYears.value.splice(idx, 1)
  else expandedYears.value.push(year)
}
```

Template year section:
```vue
<div v-for="group in timelineGroups" :key="group.year" class="timeline-year">
  <div class="year-header" @click="toggleYear(group.year)">
    <span class="year-dot"></span>
    <h2 class="year-title">{{ group.year }} 年</h2>
    <span class="year-count">{{ group.count }} 张</span>
    <span class="year-arrow" :class="{ open: isYearExpanded(group.year) }">▶</span>
  </div>
  <template v-if="isYearExpanded(group.year)">
    <div v-for="mg in group.months" ...>...</div>
  </template>
</div>
```

Hmm — wrapping months in `<template v-if>` keeps DOM clean. 

Styles:
```scss
.year-header {
  cursor: pointer;
  user-select: none;
  ...
}
.year-arrow {
  margin-left: auto;
  font-size: 0.75rem;
  color: $text-muted;
  transition: transform 0.2s ease;
  &.open {
    transform: rotate(90deg);
  }
}
```

Note: `.year-header` is flex with gap 12 — arrow with margin-left:auto pushes it right. 

Wait — one issue: the year-dot is `position: absolute; left: -33px` relative to .timeline-year. The header is inside .timeline-year — fine, unchanged.

Also when collapsed, should there be a subtle preview? Keep it simple per the request.

Let me apply the edits to TimelineView.vue.

